text:
The probe answered it cleanly, and it's not the "theory was wrong" outcome — it's "the fix was one layer too high in the stack."

```
VP: play started ret=0
VP: record started, entering loop
        <- zero messages, ever
```

Capture is armed and never clocks. Here's the register-level proof I was missing before:

`I2S_TX_START` is set in **exactly one place** — [esp32s3_i2s.c:704-709](D:/openVela/nuttx/arch/xtensa/src/esp32s3/esp32s3_i2s.c#L704-L709) — inside the DMA setup that pops a buffer off `priv->tx.pend`. `I2S_RX_START` likewise only at [:771](D:/openVela/nuttx/arch/xtensa/src/esp32s3/esp32s3_i2s.c#L771). **Submitting a buffer is what starts the clock**, not starting the device. `AUDIOIOC_START` on the play node runs [es8311_start()](D:/openVela/nuttx/drivers/audio/es8311.c#L1403-L1512), which writes codec registers and spawns a worker thread — then `es8311_processbegin()` finds `pendq` empty and issues no `I2S_SEND`. So TX_START is never set, no BCLK/WS is generated, and RX (which borrows that clock via `I2S_SIG_LOOPBACK`) shifts nothing forever.

That's why `ret=0` on both starts and still zero messages. The deadlock diagnosis holds exactly; patch #6 just didn't reach the register that matters.

**Patch #7** — prime TX with one silent buffer before starting play, which is the driver's own documented "priming the pump" path ([es8311.c:1732-1736](D:/openVela/nuttx/drivers/audio/es8311.c#L1732-L1736)):

```bash
cd ~/openvela

python3 - <<'PY'
import io
p = 'apps/system/nxlooper/nxlooper.c'
s = io.open(p, encoding='utf-8').read()

if 'VelaPaw patch #7' in s:
    print('patch #7 already applied'); raise SystemExit

old = """#ifdef CONFIG_AUDIO_MULTI_SESSION
  ret = ioctl(plooper->playdev_fd, AUDIOIOC_START,
              (unsigned long)plooper->pplayses);
#else
  ret = ioctl(plooper->playdev_fd, AUDIOIOC_START, 0);
#endif
  if (ret < 0)
    {
      goto err_out;
    }

  auderr("VP: play started ret=%d\\n", ret);"""

new = """  /* VelaPaw patch #7: submitting a buffer is what sets I2S_TX_START
   * (esp32s3_i2s.c:709); AUDIOIOC_START alone only writes codec registers
   * and spawns the worker thread.  Prime TX with one silent buffer so the
   * module actually generates the BCLK/WS that RX borrows via
   * I2S_SIG_LOOPBACK.  Enqueueing before start is the driver's own
   * "priming the pump" path -- priv->mq is still NULL, so the buffer just
   * lands on pendq and is drained by the start below.
   */

  apb = (FAR struct ap_buffer_s *)dq_remfirst(&playdq);
  if (apb != NULL)
    {
      memset(apb->samp, 0, apb->nmaxbytes);
      apb->nbytes  = apb->nmaxbytes;
      apb->curbyte = 0;
      ret = nxlooper_enqueueplaybuffer(plooper, apb);
      auderr("VP: primed TX with silence ret=%d\\n", ret);
      if (ret != OK)
        {
          goto err_out;
        }
    }

""" + old

assert s.count(old) == 1, 'FAIL: play-start anchor missing / not unique'
io.open(p, 'w', encoding='utf-8').write(s.replace(old, new))
print('patch #7 applied')
PY

grep -n "primed TX with silence" apps/system/nxlooper/nxlooper.c
./build.sh esp32s3-devkit:waveshare_lcd -j8
ls -l nuttx/nuttx.bin && md5sum nuttx/nuttx.bin
```

Then start the HTTP server and I'll pull and flash.

**On the abort call** — I said case 2 meant stopping, so this is your decision, not mine to quietly extend. My honest read is that the evidence changed underneath that commitment: I now have a single named register that is provably never written and a three-line patch that writes it, rather than another hypothesis. That's a materially better position than the one I offered to abort from. But it is still one more build/flash cycle on a stretch feature, with the enclosure assembly and the JLC reorders outstanding — if you'd rather bank it, say so and I'll reflash `nuttx-audio-step2.bin` and move to those. The root cause is written into [AUDIO_PLAN.md](D:/openVela/contest2026_043_CircuitForge/docs/AUDIO_PLAN.md) either way.

