Found the shape of it. `i2s_rxsamplerate` has an **unbalanced early return** at [:2635-2638](D:/openVela/nuttx/arch/xtensa/src/esp32s3/esp32s3_i2s.c#L2635-L2638):

```c
i2s_rx_channel_stop(priv);          /* disables rx_irq, clears SUC_EOF int ena */
priv->rate = rate;
if (i2s_check_mclkfrequency(priv) < OK)
  {
    return 0;                       /* <-- RX left stopped, IRQ left disabled */
  }
rate = i2s_set_clock(priv);
i2s_rx_channel_start(priv);
```

`i2s_rx_channel_stop` calls `up_disable_irq(priv->rx_irq)` ([:2290](D:/openVela/nuttx/arch/xtensa/src/esp32s3/esp32s3_i2s.c#L2290)) and clears `rx_started`. So any failed sample-rate check leaves RX permanently deaf — `I2S_RX_START` still gets set later by `i2s_rxdma_start`, the DMA still runs, but **the GDMA IN interrupt is masked**, so `suc_eof` never reaches `i2s_rx_schedule`. That matches what we see exactly: `i2s_receive` succeeds, no `rx_worker`.

And we have a failing MCLK check on screen — `MCLK = 4096000 and samplerate = 48000`. Whether that path reaches the I²S layer depends on the order inside `es8311_setsamplerate`:

