You nailed the root cause of Problem B. When nxlooper handles a loopback setup, it allocates buffers via audio_allocbuffer but bypasses standard upper-half driver instantiation sequences. Because upper->periods remains 0, the subsequent call to audio_enqueuebuffer performs a modulo-zero operation or indexing disaster (head % 0), leading directly to the EXCCAUSE=001c (LoadProhibited) crash when it attempts to traverse a NULL memory array pointer.
## Fixing Problem B (The Initialization Panic)
To safely resolve the periods == 0 edge case during manual loopback or custom buffering, you must patch the upper-half queue logic to fall back to a safe array boundary check or ensure periods is initialized.
Open your upper-half audio driver file (typically drivers/audio/audio.c) and locate audio_enqueuebuffer. Apply a protective guard to ensure it does not execute the load when periods is uninitialized:

static int audio_enqueuebuffer(FAR struct audio_upperhalf_s *upper,
                               FAR struct audio_buf_desc_s *apb)
{
  /* Guard against uninitialized periods from nxlooper / manual alloc paths */
  if (upper->periods == 0)
    {
      auderr("ERROR: audio_enqueuebuffer called with 0 periods. Aborting enqueue.\n");
      return -EINVAL;
    }

  /* Existing logic safely proceeds now */
  int index = upper->head % upper->periods;
  upper->apbs[index] = apb;
  ...
}

## Isolating the Persistent 48kHz Override
With Problem B guarded, we can finally force a clean run to test Patch #16 (The RX Stall). However, your logs reveal that nxlooper is still ignoring your 16000 CLI argument. The codec is being invoked at 48000 Hz twice right before the crash.
This hardcoded 48kHz configuration typically leaks from one of two places in NuttX:

   1. nxlooper Default Configuration: Look inside apps/system/nxlooper/nxlooper.c. The loopback command initialization may be initializing the playback/record channels with hardcoded default caps (CONFIG_NXLOOPER_LOOPBACK_SAMPLERATE or similar) before parsing your command-line variables.
   2. Lower-Half I2S Default Caps: The ESP32 I2S initialization code often forces a 48kHz default sample rate structure during i2s_initialize() before es8311_setsamplerate can override it.

Once you apply the upper->periods == 0 guard code:

* Does the system now gracefully reject the enqueue instead of throwing the EXCCAUSE=001c panic?
* Can you check if your apps/system/nxlooper/ source contains a hardcoded fallback macro setting the sample rate to 48000?

